## Pipes and redirects

Some of the power of Linux comes from the ability to string commands together to form "pipelines".
With the `|` pipe symbol, the output of one command can be used as the input for a second command. The command left of the pipe symbol will feed into the one on the right of the pipe symbol. 
\
For example, if we would want to count the number of lines in a gzipped file, this can be done by first using the `zcat` to get the uncompressed content of the file, and then piping the output of `zcat` into `wc` to count the lines and characters within the file:

``` bash
$ cd ~/Intro_to_Linux/yeast_files/

$ zcat raw_yeast_rnaseq_data.fastq.gz | wc
```

If you would simply run `wc` on the file, you will get a result, however it will be nonsense as `wc` doesn't know how to decrompress the file before counting the content. You might think that the `wc` command should be enhanced to work on gzipped files, but with pipes there is no need; you can simply join the commands together to make your own. 
\
\
In the case we  want to save the results of a command into a file, we can use redirects with the `>` symbol.Rather than printing results to the terminal, this symbol will write to the filename given after the `>` symbol. \
For example, we want to create a file named `my_commands.txt` that contains the text "My commands:", followed by our command history. 
To be able to create this, we need to add the first line which is "My commands:" to a new file. This can be done using the `echo` command, which simply prints out the text that you give it. We then need to redirect the results of the command into a file. 

``` bash
$ echo "My commands:" > my_commands.txt
```

:::: challenge
<h2><i class="fas fa-pencil-alt"></i> Challenge:</h2>

Check if the file has been created and the content of the file is what we redirected into it. 

<details>

<summary>Solution</summary>

::: solution
<h2><i class="far fa-eye"></i> Solution:</h2>

Remember that we can use a `cat` or `less` to take a look at the content of a file.

``` bash
$ cat my_commands.txt
```
:::

</details>
::::
\

Now that we have the header set, we need to add the content of our command history. We can get our history using the `history` command. Using the `>` we can redirect the output to a file. 


``` bash
$ history > my_commands.txt

$ head my_commands.txt
```

It seems that the file has been overwritten and the redirect has removed our originally added header. With redirects, Linux overwrites existing files without warning. Luckily, there is a way to add to an existing file without overwriting it, using `>>`.

``` bash
$ echo "My commands:" > my_commands.txt

$ history >> my_commands.txt

$ head my_commands.txt
```

Now, the original content has been kept and the history of commands has been added to the file. 

:::: key-points
<h2><i class="fas fa-thumbtack"></i> Key points</h2>

There are several different options to redirect output. 

- `>` redirects output, overwriting if a file exists
- `>>` redirects output and adds the results to an existing file
- `2>` will redirect errors only, overwriting if a file exists
- `2>>` will redirect errors only and adding errors to an existing file
- `2>&1` redirects errors to the terminal screen

:::
\

## Exercises

Below are a number of exercises exploring files and redirection. 

:::: exercise
<h2><i class="fa fa-pencil-alt"></i> Exercise 1:</h2>

Go into the yeast_files folder. Save the first 20 lines of the `raw_yeast_rnaseq_data.fastq.gz` to a file named `header.txt`, without unpacking the gzipped file.

<details>
<summary>Solution</summary>
::: solution
<h2><i class="far fa-eye"></i> Solution:</h2>

The first thing we should do is extract the content of the gzipped file. With `zcat` we can print the whole file to the terminal, we can pipe this into the `head` command to extract the first lines, remember that we can change the number of lines we want with `-n`. And finally we can redirect the results into a file.  

``` bash
$ cd ~/Intro_to_Linux/yeast_files

$ zcat raw_yeast_rnaseq_data.fastq.gz | head -n 20 > header.txt
```
:::
</details>
::::
\

:::: exercise
<h2><i class="fa fa-pencil-alt"></i> Exercise 2:</h2>

Extract the lines starting with an `@` from the gzipped file. Print the last 10 lines to screen.

<details>
<summary>Solution</summary>
::: solution
<h2><i class="far fa-eye"></i> Solution:</h2>

We can search within a gzipped file using `zgrep`. To search for lines starting with an `@` we have to use the `^` symbol. Once we have these extracted, to extract the last 10 lines we can use `tail`. By default, it takes the last 10 lines.

``` bash
$ zgrep "^@" raw_yeast_rnaseq_data.fastq.gz | tail
```
:::
</details>
::::
\

:::: exercise
<h2><i class="fa fa-pencil-alt"></i> Exercise 3:</h2>

Extract the lines starting with an `@` from the gzipped file. Print the first and the last 10 lines to a file called `names.txt`.

<details>
<summary>Solution</summary>
::: solution
<h2><i class="far fa-eye"></i> Solution:</h2>

We can search within a gzipped file using `zgrep`. To search for lines starting with an `@` we have to use the `^` symbol. Once we have these extracted, to extract the last 10 lines we can use `tail`. By default, it takes the last 10 lines.

``` bash
$ zgrep "^@" raw_yeast_rnaseq_data.fastq.gz | head > names.txt

$ zgrep "^@" raw_yeast_rnaseq_data.fastq.gz | tail >> names.txt
```
:::
</details>
